  critique: The render now passes both acceptance tests and closes all three carried-over gaps: TOP shows all eight blue BRAM banks, eight copper DSP multipliers as a clean orange column, the symmetric add/sub clusters, the two distinct reduction members (cylindrical Barrett spine + boxy shift/subtract), the right-edge controllers and both white FIFOs; FRONT/ISO show the exploded butterfly lane on its two risers and the seven-fin exploded heatsink, and the return bus carries a clear arrowhead into the crossbar. The remaining weakness is a dataflow asymmetry: only the operand-return path is drawn as a visible bus, so the twiddle-broadcast and crossbar-read paths are inferable only from the connection graph, not from the silhouette. A second nit is that adder and subtractor members are materially identical, so a viewer cannot tell sum from difference at a glance even though the build test wants them labelled.
  + Added a brass Twiddle Broadcast Rail running the full depth of the multiplier column (y=1.65) so the 'fetched once, broadcast to all eight multipliers' claim is now visible geometry rather than only a logical edge.
  + Added a brass Twiddle Broadcast Feeder linking the twiddle-factor ROM to the rail with a continuous abutting path (ROM west face -> rail T-junction), avoiding any floating segment.
  + Wired twiddle_rom -> twiddle_feeder -> twiddle_rail -> all eight DSP multipliers and rooted the new members through the ROM so the connection graph stays single-rooted with no dangling ids.
  + Updated assembly_instructions step 7 to sequence the broadcast feeder/rail install between the ROM and the address-generation unit.
  · gap: Draw the crossbar-read path as a third visible bus (crossbar -> multiplier column inputs) to complete the read/twiddle/return dataflow triad; right now only twiddle and return are rendered, so the operand-read half of the conflict-free RMW loop is still invisible in ISO.
  · gap: Give the eight subtractor members a distinct material (e.g. brushed steel vs the adders' dark anodized aluminum) so sum vs difference is readable at a glance without losing the 'symmetric pair' grouping.
  · gap: Consider nudging the exploded butterfly baseplate +Z by ~1.2 m so it no longer sits directly over its source lane in the TOP plan view, where it currently occludes lanes 2-4.
